      <article class="refactor-prose"><p class="opening">The mathematical articles here develop representations that make a specific task tractable. They show how to find the working objects, derive their allowed moves, prove what those moves preserve, and recover an answer to the original question. A mechanism calculus gives this work an explicit form. Its objects and rules must explain how the calculation reaches its conclusion.</p>
<p>The aim is to put methods within a reader’s reach. Reconstruct the choices behind an argument, compare formulations, and work through what changes when an assumption changes. An account should equip the reader to use, test and extend the construction. Sources identify the work it depends on and distinguish established results from the explanatory reconstruction offered here.</p>
<h2 id="a-different-working-object">Invent the working object</h2>
<p>A weighted difference can turn a coupled recurrence into an autonomous update. A folded graph can turn subgroup membership into a path test. A suitable coordinate change can expose a cubic obstruction hidden in quadratic equations.</p>
<h2 id="invent-the-ansatz">Invent the form that could make it work</h2>
<p>An <em>ansatz</em> is a proposed form whose coefficients or rules remain to be determined. A weighted difference, a backward move, a correction term, or a combinatorial state becomes useful when its design answers the difficulty in the original problem. Ask what the form must preserve, what it must cancel, and whether those demands can actually be met.</p>
<p>The exposition reconstructs <a href="/refactors/cayley-hamilton">Straubing’s combinatorial proof of Cayley–Hamilton</a>, as presented by Zeilberger, from a simple observation. A long walk must revisit a vertex. Cutting out the intervening cycle shortens the walk but loses its weight, so keep the cycle in a reserve. A first attempt at pairing these objects can fail to undo itself. Requiring a common unchanged prefix leads to the first-obstruction rule. The determinant expansion identifies the correction coefficients, while the unchanged prefix makes the exchange reversible. Together these steps prove the characteristic-polynomial identity.</p>
<p>Choosing a promising form requires judgment. Explain what suggested the candidate, what ruled out a simpler attempt, and which equations determined the remaining choices. These reasons make the search intelligible. The proof must then establish that the finished construction works.</p>
<h2 id="earn-the-simple-step">What the calculation establishes</h2>
<p>An existence proof or classification can completely answer the question asked. Finding a witness or locating the class of a given input may require a further procedure. An obstruction group describes where a failure can live. Computing the failure requires a representative. The question determines which output is needed and when the argument is complete. State what the argument supplies and what work remains for the next question.</p>
<p>In numerical work, the bridge may be an error bound, a preservation inequality, or a probability guarantee under stated assumptions. The retained information must support the required answer at the required accuracy. Finite-precision arithmetic, implementation choices and the final check all enter that obligation.</p>
<p>The passage from finite calculations to an infinite target also needs a proved relation across scales. <a href="/essays/continuity-as-organized-discrete-data"><em>Continuity as Organized Discrete Data</em></a> follows that requirement from rational approximations to a finite-element solve.</p>
<p>A representation can make one step cheaper while making the full computation more expensive. Count its construction, application, checking, repair and recovery costs over the intended workload. The <a href="/essays/ai-research-and-reliable-numerical-computation#earning-reliability">research essay on reliable computation</a> follows these requirements through concrete numerical methods.</p>
<h2 id="read-the-transformation">Read the transformation</h2>
<ol class="refactor-practice"><li><strong>Locate the difficulty.</strong> Identify the coupling, ambiguity, or residual that stops the argument.</li><li><strong>Invent the state.</strong> Choose what to retain so that the difficulty can be addressed.</li><li><strong>Derive the moves.</strong> Calculate how the state changes and how successive moves compose.</li><li><strong>Establish the bridge.</strong> Prove what an output in the new calculus says about the original question.</li><li><strong>Execute and test.</strong> Construct, reduce, compare, or detect failure. Identify the hypotheses and the relevant cost.</li></ol>
<h2 id="research-in-incidence-geometry">See the method in original research</h2>
<p>In my <a href="/papers/taiko-girth-obstruction">work on Taiko product structures</a>, the Garg–Mineyev triple-girth assembly route over the two-element field becomes a finite incidence problem. Coarse counts leave apparently feasible color sizes. Pressure and intersection constraints expose the incompatibility, giving a global obstruction to that construction route. The companion page follows the argument from the board to the incidence structure and back to the original question.</p>
<p>The broader direction is to find working representations in incidence geometry, graph theory, and matrix methods that retain the information a conclusion needs. <a href="/essays/ai-research-and-reliable-numerical-computation"><em>Abstraction is no refuge from AI</em></a> argues for developing this practice in the diagnosis and repair of sparse numerical representations, where approximation and total computational cost must also be controlled.</p>
<p>These demands apply to human, automated and collaborative work. The essay proposes a research direction and a test of the additional contribution supplied by specialists. Their guidance must be compared with equally equipped agents and generalists, including the cost of producing and checking it. A larger specialist advantage here than in structural mathematics remains a comparison to test. Useful methods can emerge from any of these arrangements, and their merit turns on what they establish and deliver.</p>
<h2 id="when-the-repair-stops">Make failure calculable too</h2>
<p>A refactor can reveal that the requested change is impossible. The <a href="/refactors/coordinate-repair#the-unrepairable-term">formal-coordinate example</a> seeks labels that turn multiplication near one into addition. In characteristic three, the quadratic repair succeeds while every cubic correction leaves the degree-three error intact.</p>
<p>For two commuting operators with fixed actions on an invariant subspace and its quotient, one linear map records <a href="/calculi/coupled-operators#compatibility">compatible couplings</a>. Another records the couplings that a <a href="/calculi/coupled-operators#coordinate-move">change of lift</a> can remove. Multiplying the block matrices derives both maps and shows why changing the lift preserves compatibility. A compatible coupling represents zero in the resulting quotient exactly when a common change of lift splits both operators.</p>
<h2 id="judgment-that-shows-its-work">Make the judgment answerable</h2>
<p>Judgments about mathematical work have obligations of their own. Correctness turns on a proof under stated hypotheses. Importance, clarity and promise also require informed comparisons and reasons that can be challenged. Calling a result trivial before comparing its hypotheses replaces examination with a performance of rank. The author is then left to supply the comparison and repair the impression.</p>
<p>A pure mathematician’s prestige makes that shortcut more consequential. It increases the obligation to explain a dismissal and correct a mistaken one. <a href="/essays/purity-prestige-and-pettiness#authority-that-can-answer"><em>Purity, Prestige, and Pettiness</em></a> examines this asymmetry, with Zeilberger’s public self-correction and Nathanson’s insistence on inspectable arguments as central examples of the standard it demands.</p>
<p>The same attention to what an account makes available connects this critique with exposition and learning. A correction should locate an error or equip someone to proceed. An explanation should make a method available beyond the circle that already knows it. The essays on <a href="/essays/what-a-new-proof-makes-possible">what another proof makes possible</a> and <a href="/essays/mathematical-life-in-the-age-of-ai#becoming-capable">how mathematical capability develops</a> pursue those questions in their own settings.</p>
<span id="after-the-theorem-dump" class="legacy-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2 id="the-new-hierarchy">Earlier work and current reading</h2>
<p>The <a href="/expositions">current expositions</a> and <a href="/calculi">mechanism calculi</a> develop their constructions on the page. The earlier long studies and original source editions remain in the <a href="/archive?kind=studies">Tensorgami archive</a> for readers pursuing their broader settings.</p>
